  "query_preview": "..(After midnight Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 611 AM EST SAT NOV 28 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Sunday evening with widespread rain as an\nupper-level trough attempts to become negatively tilted across the\nregion in model guidance. Showers will slowly diminish heading into\nthe late evening and overnight hours. These showers will be\nconvective in nature; however, instability remains marginal at best.\nThat said, an isolated thunderstorm or two can't be ruled out in the\nearly evening from the Lake Cumberland up through the Bluegrass \nareas. The SPC has expanded their Marginal Risk for Sunday into \nMonday morning to include the same areas where thunderstorms are \npossible. The primary threat will be isolated severe wind gusts \nwithin any thunderstorms. Aided by the strong dynamics of the \ntrough and a stout 50-70 kt low-level jet at 850 mb, winds aloft \nwill be booking it. In fact, ECMWF ENS winds fall within the \n97.5th to 99th percentile for this level when compared against \nclimatological norms.\n\nAn analysis of theta-e and wind profiles suggests access to 45 kt\n(roughly 50 mph) winds at the top of the boundary layer in both \nRAP and GFS time-height guidance. A rule of thumb is that roughly \n80% of the strongest available winds can reasonably be expected to\nreach the surface, which would support localized peak gusts \nreaching the 40-45 mph range. Separate from the isolated \nconvective threat, these gusts are expected outside of convective\nshowers, occurring along and immediately behind the cold frontal \npassage. The REFS further supports this line of thinking, with the\n00Z REFS Grand Ensemble showing much of the area with a 35-50% \nchance of wind gusts of 40 mph or greater, specifically around or \na couple of hours after midnight Sunday night. Additionally, the \n00Z LREF Grand Ensemble has a probability of gusts of 30 mph or \ngreater ranging from 35-85%, with the highest probabilities of \n85-100% near and along the high terrain of Harlan County. With the\nabove information, forecaster confidence was high enough to \ndeviate from the NBM's 20-30 mph wind gusts Sunday evening/ \novernight in favor of widespread 30-40 mph gusts using a blend of \nraw model data, while keeping in mind that isolated gusts into the\n40-45 mph range remain possible, especially along higher \nridgetops.\n\nTemperatures Sunday night were also adjusted. The NBM during this\nperiod appears to have a cold bias due to the time-lagged bias\ncorrection factoring in good radiative cooling conditions from\nrecent weeks. However, the area will remain well mixed with \nlingering cloud cover as a cold front passes through between 00Z \nand 06Z Monday (Sunday evening into early Monday morning). \nBlending toward raw model guidance yields Sunday night lows mostly \nin the lower 40s, compared to the upper 30s from the NBM.\n\nHigh pressure builds back across the Ohio Valley behind the front on\nMonday and settles over the central Appalachians by Tuesday,\nbringing a stretch of dry weather through much of the new workweek.\nBehind the departing trough, an amplifying upper-level ridge builds\nover the Southeast and Ohio Valley through midweek, allowing\ntemperatures to moderate Monday through Thursday, with highs warming\nfrom the mid 40s to the mid 60s by the end of the week. With\nclearing skies, light winds, and high pressure building back into\nthe area Monday night, valleys are expected to decouple overnight.\nEven with prime radiative cooling conditions expected, the NBM still\nruns too cold due to a lingering bias from previous strong radiative\ncooling nights. Monday night lows were produced by taking the 50th\npercentile of model guidance before adding local ridge-valley splits\nto the area. After the edits, low temperatures are expected to range\nfrom the upper 20s to low 30s along ridgetops to the mid 20s in the\nvalleys.\n\nMultiple models continue to resolve another upper-level low moving\nonshore near the California coast by Tuesday. By Wednesday, this low\nwill dig into the Four Corners region before ejecting east across\nthe southern Plains on Thursday. However, with the amplified ridge\nholding firm over the Ohio Valley and Southeast, guidance has slowed\nthe system's ejection speed, which could push the next round of\nprecipitation to late Thursday night through Friday.",
